en it must be white. But we don't have a premise that says all round things are big. So maybe Bob's size isn't determined yet.\n\nWait, but maybe we can use the fact that all nice things are quiet (premise 6). Since Bob is nice, he must be quiet (from premise 6). But Bob is round. From premise 10: If something is big and white, then it's round. But Bob is round. So if Bob is big and white, he must be round. But he's already round. So maybe Bob is white. But how do we know?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the problem is that we need to use the fact that all nice things are quiet (premise 6). So if Bob is nice, he is quiet. But Bob is round. From premise 10: If something is big and white, then it's round. But Bob is round. So if Bob is big and white, he must be round. But he's already round. So maybe Bob is white. But the problem doesn't state that. Wait, maybe the color is derived from the combination of being big and white. But how?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the color is a separate attribute. Let's consider colors:\n\n- Red (point 6): Erin is red.\n- Young (point 7): Gary is young.\n- Big (point 5): Erin is big.\n- Nice (from point 1, 8, 11): Bob is nice, Dave is round (so from premise 1, nice), but we need to check.\n\nWait, maybe the colors are determined by the combination of being big and white. For example, if something is big and white, it's round (from premise 10). But how does that relate to color?\n\nAlternatively, maybe the color is a separate category. Let's see:\n\n- Erin is red (point 6). So red is a color.\n- Dave's color isn't mentioned yet.\n- Gary's color isn't mentioned.\n- Bob's color isn't mentioned.\n\nBut maybe we can figure it out through the implications.\n\nWait, let's look at the statement \"Big, red things are young.\" (Point 12). So if something is big and red, it is young. So Erin is big and red (points 5 and 6), so according to point 12, Erin is young. But Erin is already established as big and red, so she's young. But we also have from point 15: \"Young, quiet things are red.\" So if Erin is young and quiet, she must be red. But she's already red. So that's consistent. Wait, but how do we know if Erin is quiet?\n\nWait, from point 6: \"Big, red things are young.\" So Erin is big and red, hence young. From point 15: \"Young, quiet things are red.\" So if Erin is young and quiet, she's red. But she's already red. So that's okay. But we don't know if Erin is quiet. How can we determine that?\n\nWait, maybe through the chain of implications. Let's see:\n\nIf something is big and red (Erin), then it is young (point 12). So Erin is young. From point 11: \"Young things are nice.\" So Erin is nice. From point 9: \"All nice, big things are white.\" So if Erin is nice and big, she must be white. But Erin is red. Therefore, there's a contradiction unless Erin is not big. Wait, but Erin is big (point 5). So this seems like a problem. Wait, maybe my reasoning is off.\n\nWait, let's step back. If something is big and red (Erin), then it is young (point 12). So Erin is young. From point 11, young things are nice, so Erin is nice. From point 9, all nice, big things are white. But Erin is big and red. So if she were nice, she would have to be white. But she's red. Therefore, this is a contradiction unless our premises are wrong. But the premises are given, so maybe we need to check our assumptions.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that we're assuming that being big and red makes something young, but maybe there's another way. Let's see:\n\nFrom point 12: \"Big, red things are young.\" So if something is both big and red, it's young. So Erin is big and red, so she's young. From point 11: \"Young things are nice.\" So Erin is nice. From point 9: \"All nice, big things are white.\" So if Erin is nice and big, she must be white. But she's red. Therefore, this is a contradiction. Which suggests that our premises might be inconsistent, but that can't be. So perhaps there's a mistake in the reasoning.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that \"All nice, big things are white\" (point 9) and \"Big, red things are young\" (point 12) together imply something. Let's see:\n\nIf something is big and red, it's young (point 12). If something is nice and big, it's white (point 9). So if a thing is both big and red, it's young, but if it's nice and big, it's white. So how can a big red thing be nice? It seems contradictory unless the premises are not all applicable. Wait, maybe the key is that \"All nice, big things are white\" and \"Big, red things are young\" are separate premises, but they might overlap. For example, if a thing is both nice and red, then it must be young (from point 12) and also white (from point 9). But that would require it to be white and red, which is impossible. Therefore, there must be no overlap between these categories. So maybe \"All nice, big things are white\" and \"Big, red things are young\" are separate, and there's no overlap. So if a thing is nice and big, it's white, but not red. If a thing is red and big, it's young, but not nice. Therefore, the color and the attribute of being big are separate. So maybe the color is determined by another part of the chain.\n\nWait, let's try to map each person step by step.\n\nStarting with Bob:\n\n- Round (point 2) \u2192 nice (point 1). So Bob is nice.\n- From point 8, all round things are nice. So that's consistent.\n- From point 10: If something is big and white, then it's round. But Bob is round. So if Bob is big and white, he must be round. But he's already round. So maybe Bob is white. But the problem doesn't state that. Alternatively, maybe Bob's color isn't determined yet.\n\nWait, maybe we need to look at the color of the people. The only people with colors mentioned are Erin (red) and Dave (we don't know). Let's see:\n\nErin is big (point 5) and red (point 6). From point 12: Big, red things are young. So Erin is young. From point 11: Young things are nice. So Erin is nice. From point 9: All nice, big things are white. But Erin 