ung (point 12). So if something is big and red, it's young.\n\nSo combining these two, if something is big and red, it's young, and if something is young and quiet, it's red. So there's a sort of overlap here. For example, if a thing is big, red, and quiet, then according to point 12, it's young, and according to point 14, if it's quiet, it's big. Wait, point 14: \"All quiet things are big.\" So if something is quiet, it's big. So if a thing is big, red, and quiet, then it's young (from point 12) and big (from point 14). But that's redundant. So maybe the key is that if something is big and red, it's young, and if something is young and quiet, it's red. So there's a chain from red to young to quiet to red. Wait, this is getting a bit tangled.\n\nLet me try to map this out with a Venn diagram or a table.\n\nLet's consider the attributes: age (young, old), size (small, big), color (red, white, other), and other attributes like nice, quiet.\n\nBut maybe it's better to use logical implications.\n\nStarting with the premises:\n\n1. All round things are nice.\n2. All nice, big things are white.\n3. If something is big and white, then it is round.\n4. Young things are nice.\n5. Big, red things are young.\n6. All nice things are quiet.\n7. All quiet things are big.\n8. Young, quiet things are red.\n\nLet's see if we can derive more.\n\nFrom premise 4 and 1: Young things are nice. From premise 11: All nice things are quiet. So young things are nice, which are quiet. Therefore, young things are both nice and quiet. From premise 15: Young, quiet things are red. Therefore, all young, quiet things are red. Since young, quiet things are red (point 15), and young, quiet things are a subset of young things (from point 4), then all young things are red. Wait, is that right?\n\nWait, point 15 says \"Young, quiet things are red.\" So if something is young and quiet, it is red. But from point 4, \"Young things are nice,\" and from point 6, \"All nice things are quiet,\" so young things are nice and quiet. Therefore, all young things are red (from point 15). So combining these, all young things are red. But from point 12: \"Big, red things are young.\" So if something is big and red, it is young. Therefore, all red things are young. But from above, all young things are red. So this creates a loop: all red things are young, and all young things are red. Therefore, red things are exactly the young things. But also, from point 12, big, red things are young. So if something is big and red, it's young. But if something is red, it's young. So red things are both red and young. Wait, that seems redundant. Maybe the key is that red things are exactly the young ones. So being red is equivalent to being young. But that can't be, because from point 15, young, quiet things are red. So if something is young and quiet, it's red. But if something is young, it's red regardless of being quiet or not. Wait, but from point 12, big, red things are young. So if something is big and red, it's young. But if something is young, it's red. So the set of red things includes all young things and also... Wait, no. If something is big and red, it's young. If something is young, it's red. So red things are exactly the union of big and red things (from point 12) and young things (from point 15). Wait, this is confusing.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the conclusion is that all red things are young, and all young things are red. So red is equivalent to young. But that would mean that being red is the same as being young, which might not be the case. Let's see:\n\nFrom point 12: Big, red things are young. So if something is big and red, it's young.\n\nFrom point 15: Young, quiet things are red. So if something is young and quiet, it's red.\n\nFrom point 4: Young things are nice.\n\nFrom point 6: All nice things are quiet.\n\nTherefore, young things are nice (point 4) and nice things are quiet (point 6). Therefore, young things are both nice and quiet. From point 15, young, quiet things are red. Therefore, all young things are red. But from point 12, big, red things are young. Therefore, all red things are young. So combining these, red things are exactly the young things. So being red is equivalent to being young. But that would mean that red is a color that's tied to being young, which might not be the case. But according to the premises, yes. So if something is red, it's young. And if something is young, it's red. So red and young are the same. But that seems odd. However, according to the premises, that's the conclusion.\n\nBut then, from point 12: Big, red things are young. So if something is big and red, it's young. But if something is young, it's red. So the set of red things includes all young things and also... Wait, no. If something is big and red, it's young. If something is young, it's red. So the set of red things is exactly the union of big and red things (from point 12) and young things (from point 15). Wait, no. If something is big and red, it's young. If something is young, it's red. So the set of red things is all things that are either big and red or young. But that would mean that red things include both big and small ones, which might not be the case. This is getting too convoluted. Maybe the key takeaway is that all red things are young, and all young things are red. So red is a color that applies to all young things, which might mean that red is only used on young things. But how does that affect Dave's color?\n\nWait, let's go back to the original question: Is Dave not red? So we need to determine if Dave's color is not red. Let's see what we know about Dave.\n\nDave is round (point 4). From premise 1, all round things are nice. Therefore, Dave is nice. From point 10: If something is big and white, it's round. But Dave is round. So if Dave is big and white, he must be round. But he's already round. So maybe Dave is white. But the problem doesn't state that. Alternatively, maybe Dave's color isn't determined yet.\n\nWait, but 