t in the first statement, \"Anne is cold,\" etc., these are adjectives. So \"not quiet\" is just another adjective. So \"Anne is not quiet\" would mean she is not silent. But in the context of the problem, maybe \"not quiet\" is a way to categorize something else. Wait, but the problem is in Chinese, perhaps, but the user has translated it. Maybe \"not quiet\" is just an adjective here. So \"Anne is not quiet\" would mean she is not silent. So perhaps \"not quiet\" is equivalent to \"not silent.\" So maybe \"not quiet\" here is just another way of saying \"not silent.\" So perhaps the problem is using \"not quiet\" as a way to describe something else. Wait, but in the problem, the main attributes are cold, furry, not quiet, kind, young, etc. So maybe \"not quiet\" is just an adjective here. So \"Anne is not quiet\" would mean she is not silent. So perhaps \"not quiet\" is equivalent to \"not silent.\" So maybe the problem is using \"not quiet\" as a way to describe something else. Wait, but in the problem, the main attributes are cold, furry, not quiet, kind, young, etc. So maybe \"not quiet\" is just an adjective here. So \"Anne is not quiet\" would mean she is not silent. So perhaps \"not quiet\" is equivalent to \"not silent.\" So maybe the problem is using \"not quiet\" as a way to describe something else. Wait, but in the problem, the main attributes are cold, furry, not quiet, kind, young, etc. So maybe \"not quiet\" is just an adjective here. So \"Anne is not quiet\" would mean she is not silent. So perhaps \"not quiet\" is equivalent to \"not silent.\" So maybe the problem is using \"not quiet\" as a way to describe something else. Wait, but in the problem, the main attributes are cold, furry, not quiet, kind, young, etc. So maybe \"not quiet\" is just an adjective here. So \"Anne is not quiet\" would mean she is not silent. So perhaps \"not quiet\" is equivalent to \"not silent.\" So maybe the problem is using \"not quiet\" as a way to describe something el