gs. Then \"All blue, smart things are furry.\" So \"Furry\" is a category that encompasses blue and smart. Then \"All smart, white things are furry.\" So if something is both smart and white, it's furry. Then \"Young things are nice.\" So if something is young, it's nice. \"If Fiona is smart and Fiona is young then Fiona is not furry.\" So if Fiona is both smart and young, she can't be furry. \"If Erin is kind then Erin is furry.\" So kind implies furry. \"If Gary is smart and Gary is white then Gary is not kind.\" So if Gary is smart and white, he's not kind. \"If something is nice then it is blue.\" So being nice implies blue.\n\nBut then Fiona is both blue and white. So according to \"If something is nice then it is blue,\" Fiona would have to be nice and blue. But Fiona is white. So that's a contradiction. Therefore, Fiona cannot be nice. But according to \"If something is nice then it is blue,\" which is statement 16. So if Fiona is nice, she must be blue. But Fiona is white. Therefore, Fiona is not nice. But according to statement 16, \"If something is nice then it is blue,\" which would be a contradiction if Fiona is not nice. Wait, maybe the contrapositive? The contrapositive of \"If something is nice, then it is blue\" is \"If something is not blue, then it is not nice.\" Which is also true. So if Fiona is not blue, then she is not nice. Which is consistent because Fiona is white. So that's okay. So Fiona is white, hence not nice. But how does that help us with Erin?\n\nErin is blue and not kind. Let's see. If something is young and blue, then it is smart (statement 9). So Erin is young and blue, so she is smart. But she is not kind. Then, if something is smart and not kind, what? There's a statement 14: \"If Erin is kind then Erin is furry.\" But since Erin is not kind, that statement doesn't tell us anything. Wait, the contrapositive would be \"If Erin is not furry, then Erin is not kind.\" But we know Erin is not kind, so that doesn't help. Hmm.\n\nWait,