blue \u2192 red \u2192 red.\n\nNow, let's look at Gary. Gary is green (statement 5). So he's green. Since green is assigned to cold but not young. So if Gary is green, he must not be young. But is he cold? Let's see. Harry is cold (statement 6) and rough (statement 7). So Harry is cold. From statement 9, if something is cold, it's green. So Harry is green. But Harry is also rough. Wait, but we don't have any info about Harry's age. So Harry is cold, so green. But is Harry young? Not necessarily. The only young person mentioned is Fiona (statements 3,4). So Fiona is young, white, and cold. So from statement 8, all young, cold things are white. Fiona is both young and cold, so she's white. That's consistent.\n\nNow, Gary is green. Since green is for cold but not young. So if Gary is green, he's not young. But is he cold? Let's see. Are there any other cold, non-young things? The only cold, young person is Fiona. So if Gary is green, he's cold but not young. But are there any other cold, non-young things? The problem doesn't mention others. So maybe Gary is cold but not young. But how does that help us determine his color? His color is green. So he's green. Now, the question is, is he blue? Let's see.\n\nWhite things are blue (statement 11). So if Gary is white, he would be blue. But Gary is green. So he's green. Therefore, he's not white. Therefore, he's not blue. So Gary is green, not blue. Therefore, the statement \"Gary is blue\" is false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, let me check again. Let's make sure there's no other way. Let's recap:\n\n- All young, cold things are white (statement 8). So if something is both young and cold, it's white.\n\n- All cold things are green (statement 9). So if something is cold, it's green.\n\n- Young things are white (statement 10). So if something is young, it's white.\n\n- White things are blue (statement 11). So if something is white, it's blue.\n\n- Therefore, if something is both young and white, it's blue. But since young things are white (statement 10), all young things are white. So all young things are white, which are blue. So all young things are blue.\n\nWait, hold on. If all young things are white (statement 10), and white things are blue (statement 11), then all young things are blue. So if something is young, it's blue. But Fiona is young (statements 3,4), so she's blue. But earlier, we thought green is for cold but not young. Wait, this seems conflicting. Let me check again.\n\nWait, maybe my earlier reasoning was wrong. Let's parse the statements again.\n\nStatement 10: Young things are white. So all young things are white.\n\nStatement 11: White things are blue. So all white things are blue.\n\nTherefore, combining these two: All young things are white, and all white things are blue. Therefore, all young things are blue. So if something is young, it's blue. But Fiona is young (statements 3,4), so she's blue. But earlier, we had that green is for cold but not young. But if all young things are blue, then green must be for cold but not young. But if all young things are blue, then green is only for cold and not young. So that's possible.\n\nWait, but statement 8 says all young, cold things are white. If all young things are blue, then how does that interact? Let's see. If something is young, it's blue. If something is cold, it's green. So if something is both young and cold, it's white (from statement 8). But if all young things are blue, then even if they are cold, they are white (blue) but also young. Wait, this is confusing.\n\nWait, maybe the problem is that the rules are not all independent. Let's try to list all the implications.\n\n1. Young \u2192 Blue (from 10 and 11)\n2. Cold \u2192 Green (from 9)\n3. Young and Cold \u2192 White (from 8)\n4. Therefore, if something is both young and cold, it's white. But if something is only young, it's blue. If something is only cold, it's green.\n\nSo, for example, Fiona is young (from 3,4) and cold (from 2). Therefore,