 is big. All big things are blue. If something is green then it is blue. Blue things are big.\"\n\nHmm, maybe statement 5 and 6 are the same? Let me parse each one carefully.\n\n1. All blue things are rough. (B \u2192 R)\n2. All white things are kind. (W \u2192 K)\n3. G \u2227 K \u2192 R\n4. R \u2192 B (if Anne is rough, then she's big)\n5. B \u2192 B (All big things are blue) \u2013 Wait, this seems redundant. Maybe a mistranslation? The original might have been \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B? That doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe it's supposed to be \"All big things are blue,\" which would be B \u2192 B, which is a tautology. That can't be. Wait, perhaps there's a typo here. Let me check the original problem again.\n\nOriginal problem's statements:\n\n- \"All blue things are rough.\" (B \u2192 R)\n- \"All white things are kind.\" (W \u2192 K)\n- \"If something is green and kind then it is round.\" (G \u2227 K \u2192 R)\n- \"If Anne is rough then Anne is big.\" (R \u2192 B)\n- \"All big things are blue.\" (B \u2192 B) \u2013 Hmm, this seems like a mistake. Maybe it's supposed to be \"All big things are blue,\" which would be B \u2192 B, which is trivial. Alternatively, maybe \"All blue things are big,\" which would be B \u2192 B? No, that's still trivial. Wait, perhaps the original statement 5 was supposed to be \"All big things are blue,\" but phrased differently. Alternatively, maybe it's \"All blue things are big,\" which would be B \u2192 B? That still doesn't make sense. Wait, maybe there's a translation error here. Let me check the original problem again.\n\nWait, the user provided the context as:\n\n\"Please solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. ##\n# Context:\nAnne is big. Anne is blue. Anne is kind. Anne is round. Charlie is green. Gary is kind. Harry is round. All blue things are rough. All white things are kind. If something is green and kind then it is round. If Anne is rough then Anne is big. All big things are blue. If something is green then it is blue. Blue things are big.\"\n\nHmm, maybe statement 5 is \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B? That can't be. Wait, maybe it's a typo and should be \"All blue things are big,\" which would be B \u2192 B? No, that's still a tautology. Alternatively, maybe \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B? That still doesn't make sense. Wait, perhaps the original problem had a different structure. Let me try to parse each statement again.\n\nWait, maybe statement 5 is \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's a problem because it's a tautology. Alternatively, maybe it's \"All blue things are big,\" which would be B \u2192 B. Hmm, this is confusing. Let me check the original problem once more.\n\nOriginal problem's statements:\n\n1. All blue things are rough. (B \u2192 R)\n2. All white things are kind. (W \u2192 K)\n3. G \u2227 K \u2192 R\n4. R \u2192 B\n5. All big things are blue. (B \u2192 B)\n6. Green \u2192 blue\n7. Blue \u2192 big\n\nWait, maybe statement 5 is \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's a problem. Alternatively, maybe statement 5 is \"All blue things are big,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's still a tautology. This seems like a mistake in the problem statement. Alternatively, maybe statement 5 is \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B. That can't be. Wait, maybe the original problem had a different structure. Let me check again.\n\nWait, perhaps statement 5 is \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's a problem. Alternatively, maybe it's \"All blue things are big,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's still a tautology. This is confusing. Maybe there's a mistake in the problem's translation. Alternatively, maybe statement 5 is \"All big things are blue,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's a problem. Alternatively, maybe it's \"All blue things are big,\" which is B \u2192 B. That's still a tautology. Hmm.\n\nWait,