ected?\n\nAlternatively, maybe through Fiona. Point 11 says if Fiona is furry, then Fiona is blue. But Erin is blue (point 3). If Erin were furry, then according to point 11, she would be blue. But Erin is already blue. Wait, that might not necessarily be a contradiction. Let me think.\n\nWait, point 11 is a conditional: If something is furry, then it is blue. So if Erin were furry, then she would have to be blue. But Erin is already blue. So that doesn't create a contradiction. It just means that if something is both furry and blue, it must be something else? Wait, no. The conditional is just a statement; it doesn't say that all blue things are furry. Wait, no. Let me check again.\n\nPoint 11: **If Fiona is furry then Fiona is blue.** So this is a one-way implication. If Fiona is furry, then she must be blue. But it doesn't say that all furry things are blue. Similarly, if something is blue, it doesn't necessarily have to be furry. So Erin is blue, but that doesn't make her furry unless there's a reason. But according to the previous chain, she must be furry because she's rough and cold.\n\nAlternatively, let's look at the statement that all quiet, big things are not round (point 9). But Erin is not quiet (as established earlier), so point 9 doesn't affect her. Similarly, point 10 says all quiet, blue things are big. But Erin is blue and not quiet, so that doesn't apply.\n\nAnother angle: If something is big (point 1 and 10), then it is cold (point 13). So all big things are cold. Since Erin is not big (we don't have a direct statement about her size, but Dave is big. Wait, the context says \"Dave is big\" but doesn't mention Erin's size. So we don't know if Erin is big. Hmm, that complicates things.\n\nWait, point 10 says \"All quiet, blue things are big.\" But Erin is blue (point 3) and not quiet (as established), so she doesn't fall under point 10. So we can't conclude she's big based on that. So maybe Erin's size is unknown. But the question is about if she's f