le are rough.\" So that doesn't say anything about cold people who are not white. So if someone is cold but not white, they might not be rough. But in our case, we have to determine if Bob is white.\n\nWait, let's recap. From the initial statements:\n\n- Bob is nice (given).\n- Therefore, Bob is big (from \"If nice, then big\").\n- Therefore, Bob is red (from \"All big people are red\").\n- Therefore, Bob is rough (from \"All red, big people are rough\").\n- Therefore, Bob is furry (from \"All rough people are furry\").\n- Therefore, Bob is cold (from \"All furry, big people are cold\").\n- Therefore, since Bob is cold, according to the statement \"All cold, white people are rough,\" he must be white (because if he's cold, he must be white to satisfy the roughness condition).\n\nWait, but the statement is a conditional: If someone is rough and cold, then they are white. But here, we have someone who is cold (Bob) and we need to determine if he's white. But according to the chain of reasoning, Bob is rough and cold, so by the conditional statement, he must be white. So that would make Bob white.\n\nBut let's check if there's any contradiction here. If Bob is white, then according to the statement \"All cold, white people are rough,\" which would be true because Bob is both cold and white and also rough. So that's consistent.\n\nAlternatively, if Bob were not white, then according to the conditional statement, since he's rough and cold, he should be white. So that would create a contradiction. Therefore, Bob must be white.\n\nTherefore, the statement \"Bob is not white\" would be false. So the answer should be B) False.\n\nWait, but let me double-check each step to make sure I didn't make a mistake.\n\n1. Bob is nice: given. So step 1 is correct.\n2. If nice \u2192 big: yes, so Bob is big. Step 2 correct.\n3. All big \u2192 red: given, so Bob is red. Step 3 correct.\n4. All red, big \u2192 rough: yes, so Bob is rough. Step 4 correct.\n5. All rough \u2192 furry: yes, s