en. But if a thing is red, then it's also furry (from points 8-9), so red things are green. But point 10 says young things are red. So young things are red, which are green. So young things are green? Wait, but point 13 says if something is red and green, then it is blue. So if a thing is both red and green, it's blue. But if a thing is young (from point 1) and red (from point 10), then according to point 13, that would make it blue. But point 10 says young things are red. So there's a contradiction here. Wait, maybe I made a mistake.\n\nWait, let's start over. Let's parse each point carefully.\n\n1. Charlie is young.\n\n2. Erin is blue.\n\n3. Erin is green.\n\n4. Fiona is big.\n\n5. Fiona is green.\n\n6. Gary is blue.\n\n7. Gary is green.\n\n8. All red things are furry.\n\n9. All furry things are red.\n\n10. Young things are red.\n\n11. If something is smart and not red, then it is not green.\n\n12. All furry things are green.\n\n13. If something is red and green, then it is blue.\n\n14. If something is blue and green, then it is smart.\n\n15. Blue, red things are smart.\n\nWait, point 15 says \"Blue, red things are smart.\" Maybe that's a typo? Maybe it should be \"All blue, red things are smart.\" But assuming it's correct as given, perhaps it's a translation issue. Maybe it means that blue and red things are smart. But that seems odd because from point 13, if something is red and green, it's blue. So if something is red, it's not green unless it's blue. Wait, this is getting confusing. Let's try to map out the color and smart relationships.\n\nFirst, let's note the colors:\n\n- Erin is blue (point 2) and green (point 3). Wait, that's conflicting. How can Erin be both blue and green? That's impossible unless there's a mistake. Wait, maybe that's a typo. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal context:\n\n\"Given a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C.\n\n# Context:\nCharlie is young. Erin is blue. Erin is green. Fiona is big. Fiona is green. 