nd, they are white. But Dave is rough (point 3), so he can't be round. Wait, hold on. Wait, point 13 says that furry people are round. So if someone is furry, they are round. Therefore, if someone is rough, can they be furry? Wait, no. Wait, being rough doesn't necessarily mean they are furry. Wait, point 13 says that if someone is furry, they are round. So being round is a consequence of being furry, but not the other way around. So someone could be rough and not furry. For example, maybe there are rough people who are not furry. But if someone is rough and also furry, then they are round. So if Dave is rough and furry, then he is round (from 13). Then from 8, since he's round, he is white. So even if he's not young, if he's rough and furry, he's white. So regardless of age, rough and furry implies white. Therefore, Dave is white. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nWait, but let me check if there's any other way. Suppose Dave is rough, not young, and not white. Is that possible? Let's see. If someone is rough, can they be non-white? But according to point 14, white, round people are furry. But if someone is rough and not white, can they still be furry? Wait, point 13 says that if someone is furry, they are round. So if someone is furry, they must be round. But being round doesn't necessarily make them white. Wait, point 8 says all round people are white. So if someone is round, they are white. So if someone is rough and also round, they are white. But if someone is rough and not round, they could be non-white. Wait, but point 13 says that if someone is furry, they are round. So if someone is rough, but not furry, then they are not round. So if someone is rough and not round, they are not white (since all round people are white). But if someone is rough and also furry, then they are round and white. So the key is, if someone is rough and also furry, they are white. But if someone is rough and not furry, they could be non-white. But in the context of the problem, Dave is rough. So if Dave is rough, and if he is also furry, then he is white. But if he is not furry, he could be non-white. But the problem is, can someone be rough and not furry? Let's see. The problem states that \"Furry people are round.\" So if someone is not furry, they are not round. So if someone is rough and not round, they are not white. But if someone is rough and also furry, they are round and white. So in the case of Dave, since he is rough (point 3), if he is also furry, he is white. But if he is not furry, he could be non-white. However, the problem doesn't give us any information about Dave's furriness. So is there a way for Dave to be rough and not white? Let's check the premises.\n\nFrom point 14: White, round people are furry. So if someone is white and round, they are furry. But that doesn't mean that all white people are round. Wait, no. Wait, point 8 says all round people are white. So white people can be round or not? Wait, no. Wait, point 8 says \"Round people are white.\" So that means that if someone is round, they must be white. It doesn't say that all white people are round. So white people can be non-round. But point 14 says \"White, round people are furry.\" So if someone is white and round, they are furry. But if someone is white and not round, they could be non-furry. But point 13 says \"Furry people are round.\" So if someone is not furry, they are not round. So if someone is white and not round, they are not round, so they are not furry. So in that case, white people can be non-round and non-furry. But the key is, if someone is rough, can they be non-white? If someone is rough and also not round, then they are not white (since all round people are white). But if someone is rough and also round, they are white. So the only way someone can be rough and not white is if they are rough and not round. But can someone be rough and not round? The problem doesn't specify. So is there a possib