 if someone is rough and also furry, then they are round (from 13). So combining these, if someone is rough and also furry, they must be white. So the conclusion is that if someone is both rough and furry, they are white. Therefore, Dave, if he is both rough and furry, would be white. But Dave is also not young. But the color is determined by being white, round, and furry. So regardless of age, if someone is rough and furry, they are white. Therefore, Dave must be white. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" is false. So the answer is B) False.\n\nBut wait, let's check if there's a possibility that Dave is rough and not white. Suppose Dave is rough and not white. Then, according to point 13, he is not round. But if he is not round, then according to point 8, he is not white. So that's consistent. But does that contradict any premises?\n\nWait, the problem is that if Dave is rough and not white, then he is not round. But point 13 says that if someone is furry, they are round. So if Dave is not round, he is not furry. Therefore, he cannot be furry. So if Dave is rough and not white, he is not furry. But then, how is he rough? There's no contradiction here. He could be a rough person who is not white and not furry. But according to point 14, white, round people are furry. So if someone is white and round, they are furry. But if someone is not white, they can be rough and not furry. So in that case, Dave could be rough and not white. But does that contradict any premises?\n\nWait, let's see. The premises are:\n\n1. All round people are white.\n\n2. All young people are white.\n\n3. Dave is rough.\n\n4. Dave is not young.\n\n5. Erin is cold.\n\n6. Erin is young.\n\n7. Harry is furry.\n\n8. Round people are white.\n\n9. All round, rough people are big.\n\n10. All young, big people are not cold.\n\n11. All round, big people are not cold.\n\n12. Furry people are round.\n\n13. If someone is furry, they are round.\n\n14. If someone is white and round, they are furry.\n\nSo, if Dave is rough (point 3) and not white, then he is not round (since all round people are white). Therefore, he is not round. But if he is not round, can he be rough? Yes, there's no restriction that roughness implies roundness. So he could be rough and not round. But then, according to point 13, if he is not round, he is not furry. But he is rough. So he is rough and not round, hence not furry. But then, how does that interact with other premises? There's no direct contradiction. So in that case, Dave could be rough and not white. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" would be true in that case. But wait, but according to the earlier reasoning, if someone is both rough and furry, they must be white. But if someone is rough and not furry, they can be non-white. So the problem is that the conclusion that Dave is white comes from the fact that if someone is both rough and furry, they are white. But if someone is rough and not furry, they can be non-white. So the key is, is there a way for Dave to be rough and not white? Yes, if he is not furry. But the premises don't prevent that. So in that case, Dave could be rough and not white. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" is not necessarily true. Therefore, the answer might be C) Unknown.\n\nWait, this is conflicting with my previous conclusion. Let me try to structure this.\n\nFirst, let's outline all possible attributes and relationships:\n\n1. All round people are white (point 8).\n\n2. All young people are white (point 11).\n\n3. All round, rough people are big (point 10).\n\n4. All young, big people are not cold (point 11).\n\n5. All round, big people are not cold (point 12).\n\n6. Furry people are round (point 13).\n\n7. White, round people are furry (point 14).\n\nFrom 6 and 7: If someone is both furry and round, then they are white (from 14, since white, round, and furry implies... Wait, no. Wait, point 14 says \"White, round people are furry.\" So if someone is white and round, they are furry. But point 13 says \"Furry people are round.\" So combining these, if someone is both white, round, and furry, then they are both furry and round, which would make them white. But the conclusion given is: If Dave is furry and round, then Dave is rough. So applying point 13 and 14: If someone is both furry and round, they are rough. So Dave, if he is both rough and furry, must be white. But if someone is rough and not furry, they can be non-white. So the key is, can Dave be rough and not white?\n\nYes, if he is not furry. Because there's no restriction that roughness requires being furry. So if Dave is rough and not furry, he is not white. But does that contradict any premises?\n\nLooking at the premises:\n\n- All round people are white: If someone is round, they are white. But if someone is not round, they can be non-white.\n\n- All young people are white: But Dave is not young, so this doesn't apply.\n\n- Furry people are round: So if someone is not furry, they are not round.\n\nSo if Dave is rough (not round) and not white, that's allowed. There's no contradiction. Therefore, Dave could be rough and not white. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" is not necessarily true. Therefore, the answer is C) Unknown.\n\nBut wait, the problem is that the conclusion given in the context is: If Dave is furry and round, then Dave is rough. So if Dave is both rough and furry, he must be white. But if Dave is not furry, he can be rough and not white. Therefore, the color of Dave depends on whether he is furry or not. But the problem doesn't give us any information about whether Dave is furry or not. So we can't determine for sure if Dave is white or not. Therefore, the statement \"Dave is not white\" is unknown.\n\nBut wait, let's check the premises again. The problem states that \"If someone is young then they are white.\" But Dave is not young, so that doesn't affect his color. The other premises relate to roundness, roughness, age, etc. But there's no direct 