 and nice, then it's young. But Fiona is neither rough nor nice. So that rule doesn't apply to her. The other rule \"If something is white and furry, then it is smart.\" If Fiona is white and furry, then she is smart. But again, her other attributes aren't specified. So unless we can link her being smart to something else.\n\nBut the problem is about whether Fiona is not young. So we need to see if there's any information that can tell us she's old. Let's check the rules again.\n\nLooking at the rule \"All rough, nice things are young.\" If Fiona were both rough and nice, she would be young. But Fiona's attributes don't include being nice or rough. So unless there's a way to infer that.\n\nAlternatively, the rule \"If something is white and furry, then it is smart.\" If Fiona is white and furry, then she is smart. But again, we don't know if she's smart. However, the problem doesn't state that Fiona is smart. So unless we can use the rule in reverse. If we can find something that says if someone is smart, then they are cold. But Fiona's smartness isn't given.\n\nWait, the rule \"All smart things are cold.\" So if Fiona were smart, she would be cold. But she's already stated to be cold. But again, we don't know if she's smart. So unless we can link her being smart to another attribute.\n\nAlternatively, the rule \"If something is white and furry, then it is smart.\" So if Fiona is white and furry, then she is smart. If she's smart, then according to \"All smart things are cold,\" she would be cold. But she's already cold. So that would be consistent. But if she's not smart, then the rule doesn't apply. So we can't be sure.\n\nBut the problem is about whether Fiona is not young. So let's see if there's any other way to determine her age. The rule \"Young things are cold.\" So if something is young, it is cold. But Fiona is cold. So being cold doesn't make someone young. The rule is \"Young things are cold,\" not that all cold things are young. So just being cold doesn