reen, which makes the dog kind. If the dog is kind, then maybe the squirrel is affected. Wait, not sure. Let's think.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the squirrel's roughness is related to the dog's attributes. But I don't see a direct link. Hmm. This is getting a bit tangled. Let's try to approach this methodically.\n\nFirst, list all the animals involved: Bald eagle, dog, bear, squirrel, and \"someone\" (which could be any being, but likely referring to the same animals or other entities).\n\nLet's note the attributes:\n\n- Green: Dog (from points 4 and 11)\n- Kind: Dog (from green people are kind)\n- Visits: If someone is kind, they visit the bald eagle (point 8). So if the dog is kind, it visits the bald eagle. But the dog sees the bald eagle (point 5). So maybe the dog is interested in visiting but hasn't gone yet. Not sure.\n- Sees: Dog sees bald eagle (5), dog sees bear (6), squirrel sees bear (7). So the dog can see all these, the squirrel can see the bear.\n- Likes: Dog likes the dog (point 3), dog likes the dog (point 3 again). Wait, point 3 says the bear likes the dog. So the dog is liked by the bear. The dog also likes the dog? That seems redundant. Maybe that's a translation issue. Wait, original problem says: \"The dog is green. The dog sees the bald eagle. The bear likes the dog. The dog is green. The dog sees the bald eagle. The dog sees the bear. The squirrel sees the bear. If someone is kind then they visit the bald eagle. If the dog is nice and the dog is cold then the dog sees the squirrel. If someone likes the dog and the dog is green then they like the dog. If someone visits the bald eagle and they are kind then the bald eagle is green. Green people are kind. If someone visits the bald eagle then the bald eagle sees the dog. If the dog sees the squirrel and the squirrel is rough then the squirrel likes the bald eagle.\"\n\nWait, maybe some of the points are duplicates. Let me check again.\n\nOriginal context:\n\n1. The bald eagle likes the dog.\n2. The