gle. Maybe that's redundant too. Hmm. Maybe the problem has some redundancy, but let's proceed.\n\nSo, focusing on the key points:\n\n- Dog is green (from 4 and 11: green people are kind).\n- Dog is kind (from 11).\n- If someone is kind, they visit the bald eagle (point 8). So if the dog is kind, then the dog would visit the bald eagle. But the dog sees the bald eagle (point 5). So maybe the dog is interested in visiting, but hasn't gone yet. Not sure.\n- The dog sees the bear (point 6) and the squirrel (point 7). So the dog can see all these.\n- The squirrel sees the bear (point 7). So the squirrel can see the bear.\n- If the dog is nice and cold, the dog sees the squirrel (point 9). But we don't know if the dog is nice and cold.\n- If the dog sees the squirrel (which it does) and the squirrel is rough, then the squirrel likes the bald eagle (point 10). But we don't know if the squirrel is rough.\n- Green people are kind (11). So the dog is green, hence kind.\n- If someone visits the bald eagle and they are kind (point 8), then the bald eagle is green. Wait, point 12 says if someone visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog. But point 11 says green people are kind. So if the person visiting is kind (green), then the bald eagle is green. Wait, maybe that's a different point. Let me check point 12 again: \"If someone visits the bald eagle and they are kind then the bald eagle is green.\" Wait, no, point 12 says: \"If someone visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog.\" That's the conclusion. The premise is that someone visits and is kind. Wait, no, point 12 is: \"If someone visits the bald eagle, then the bald eagle sees the dog.\" That's the entire premise. So the conclusion is the bald eagle sees the dog, which is already stated in point 5. Wait, maybe point 12 is redundant. Because point 5 already says the dog sees the bald eagle. So maybe that's a mistake. Alternatively, maybe the premise is that if someone visits the bald eagle and is